  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1218 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nHot temperatures continue throughout the Mid-South with areawide\ntemperatures still in the upper 80s and low 90s as of the time of\nwriting. Temperatures will cool through the night, but limited\nrelief is expected, especially across the Mississippi River Delta\nin Arkansas and Mississippi. A wave, currently entering northern\nArkansas, will move southeastward through the Mid-South tonight\nand tomorrow, bringing scattered showers. Forecast soundings show\nample instability (3000+ MUCAPE) for thunderstorms, but an\novernight inversion near the surface is likely to keep the\ncoverage of any convection isolated. However, through the day\nWednesday, enough insolation may erode this inhibition and allow\nfor thunderstorms to develop into the afternoon, but confidence\nin the placement and coverage of thunderstorms is still low. In\nterms of temperature, enough cloud cover should be present to\nkeep afternoon heat indices below 105 F, aside from the\naforementioned areas in the Mississippi River Delta where a new\nHeat Advisory has been hoisted through 8 PM CDT Wednesday\nevening. There is the possibility that thunderstorms could\ndevelop in this area and cut the dangerous heat before the\nexpiration of the Heat Advisory, which will be monitored through\nthe next 24 hours.\n\nBehind the weak wave Wednesday, a cold front will help reinforce\ncooler temperatures throughout the Mid-South. The broad\nnorthwesterly upper flow that has allowed for this pattern shift\nwill continue, keeping the cooler temperatures throughout the\nregion into at least this weekend. Medium range models continue\nto show a lee surface cyclone tracking along the baroclinic zone\ngenerated by the cold front late this week, eventually reaching\nthe Middle Mississippi River Valley by late Friday and will offer\nour next chance of rainfall.\n\nLooking closer into the details of the system Friday and early\nSaturday, one thing is readily apparent: PWATs will be very high.\nMultiple models (NAM/GFS/ECMWF/NBM) have PWATs approaching, if\nnot exceeding, 2.2\" as the surface wave approaches. At the same\ntime, this suite of guidance also shows elevated westerlies\nbetween 925 and 850 mb, which would act to guide this moisture\ninto and over the cooler air mass laid out by the cold front\nWednesday. This would typically lead to a signal for a zone of\nhigh rainfall accumulation, but differences in the mesoscale\nevolution between these models precludes higher confidence in\nheavy rainfall. The largest detractor from heavy rain is the\nuncertainty in instability, which may be low due to both\nrelatively dry air near the surface and weak mid-level lapse\nrates. Regardless, late July and early August have a propensity\nto produce instability from a lot less than what is present in\nthe guidance and trends may need to be monitored for heavy\nrainfall potential Friday and into Saturday.\n\nThe surface cyclone will eventually move east of the region late\nSaturday and into Sunday. Additional convection is possible along\nanother cold front as it moves south Saturday night, but severe\nweather and heavy rainfall concerns remain limited due to\ncontinued uncertainty in the thermodynamic profiles across the\nregion. Behind the front, temperatures are expected to remain in\nthe 80s Sunday and flirt with 90 into early next week. The upper\ntroughing will begin to slowly erode through the end of the\nperiod and beyond, potentially marking the return of warmer\nweather. However, current guidance continues to show reasonably\nlow dewpoints at or below 70, which should keep the forecast dry\nand less unbearable compared to this last heat wave.",
